# The stage on edges runs on swept code too, for the blocks that the sweep has reached: each ends in edges of its own, which lead to blocks that are reached. Only the blocks that the sweep has passed break those rules.
# THE LIE, testing=SweepLetsGoEdgeThatIsTaken: in each unit the edge of one reached block that ends in a jump loses its `to`, as the edges do that the sweep lets go by right.
# Without the stage the later stages took `to` unasked: the verifier itself died, and no rule was named.
# -- source: main.js
# function fill(to, x) { for (let i = 0; i < to.length; i++) to[i] = x | 0; return to; }
# print(fill([1.5, 2.5, 3.5].slice(), 7).join() + " " + fill([1, 2, 3].slice(), 7).join());
# -- end of source
# OPTIONS: check=Validate testing=SweepLetsGoEdgeThatIsTaken
# DIAGNOSTIC: OpcodeNotInForm
# RUN: compile mechanisms=all
